# 19. So what is "backpropagation"?

We can now give a very precise definition.

**Forward propagation:**

$$
\boxed{
\text{inputs}
\rightarrow
\text{neurons}
\rightarrow
\text{output}
\rightarrow
\text{loss}
}
$$

**Backpropagation:**

$$
\boxed{
\text{loss}
\rightarrow
\text{output}
\rightarrow
\text{hidden neurons}
\rightarrow
\text{parameters}
}
$$

At every step we apply the chain rule.

**Gradient descent:**

$$
\boxed{
\text{parameters}
\leftarrow
\text{parameters}
-
\text{learning rate}\times\text{gradient}
}
$$

So backpropagation and gradient descent are actually **two different things**:

* **Backpropagation** computes the gradients efficiently.
* **Gradient descent** uses those gradients to update the parameters.

That's an important distinction.

---

# 20. And now connect this to an LLM

The tiny network above had only 9 parameters.

An LLM may have billions of parameters.

But conceptually, the procedure is remarkably similar:

```text
                 FORWARD PASS

tokens
   ↓
embeddings
   ↓
transformer layers
   ↓
probabilities for next token
   ↓
loss
   │
   │
   ▼
             BACKWARD PASS

       calculate gradients
              ↓
       every parameter gets
          an error signal
              ↓
       gradient descent /
       Adam-type optimizer
              ↓
       parameters updated
```

The enormous complexity of an LLM comes from the **architecture and the sheer number of parameters**, not from a fundamentally different calculus operation.

