# 17. Where pGCL fits

This connects particularly nicely to what you've been studying.

A pGCL program can describe something like:

$$
\{P\}\ C\ \{Q\}
$$

where probabilistic commands determine the distribution of program states.

For example, a probabilistic choice might conceptually be:

```text
x := 1
```

with probability \(p\), and

```text
x := 0
```

with probability \(1-p\).

A formal pGCL analysis might ask:

$$
P(\text{program terminates})
$$

or:

$$
E[x]
$$

or establish an invariant.

Differentiable probabilistic programming asks a somewhat different question:

$$
\boxed{
\frac{\partial}{\partial p}
E_p[f(X)]
}
$$

In other words:

> **How does the quantitative behavior of the probabilistic program change when I change one of its probability parameters?**

That's a very interesting bridge between the two areas.

---

# 18. An even deeper connection: program semantics

You can think of a probabilistic program as defining a mathematical transformation:

$$
\theta
\longrightarrow
\text{probability distribution}
\longrightarrow
\text{expectation}.
$$

Symbolically:

$$
\theta
\stackrel{\text{program}}{\longrightarrow}
P_\theta
\stackrel{f}{\longrightarrow}
E_{P_\theta}[f].
$$

DPP asks whether this entire transformation is differentiable and, if so, how to compute:

$$
D_\theta
\left(
E_{P_\theta}[f]
\right).
$$

This is very close philosophically to what you're doing with formal semantics in Isabelle:

$$
\boxed{
\text{program}
\rightarrow
\text{mathematical semantics}
\rightarrow
\text{properties}
}
$$

DPP adds:

$$
\boxed{
\text{differentiate the semantics with respect to program/model parameters}
}
$$

---

# 19. And this eventually connects to LLMs

An LLM is itself a gigantic probabilistic model:

$$
P_\theta(x_1,\ldots,x_n).
$$

Training typically minimizes negative log likelihood:

$$
L(\theta)
=
-E_{\text{data}}
[\log P_\theta(x)].
$$

Then:

$$
\nabla_\theta L
$$

is calculated by backpropagation.

There is an important distinction, though:

**ordinary neural-network training generally doesn't require sampling a discrete token and differentiating through that sampled token.**

During training, the model calculates a softmax probability distribution over the vocabulary and the cross-entropy loss is differentiable.

That's why LLM training is comparatively friendly to automatic differentiation.

But if you build a probabilistic program where the model makes **discrete stochastic choices during computation**, you encounter exactly the difficulties we discussed above.

