# 7. There is already research connecting these worlds

This isn't merely a philosophical connection.

Recent work uses **neural networks inside quantitative verification**. For example, one approach trains a neural network to represent a certificate for probability bounds, then formally checks the resulting certificate with SMT solving. The process is essentially:

$$
\text{neural training}
\rightarrow
\text{candidate certificate}
\rightarrow
\text{formal verification}
\rightarrow
\text{counterexample}
\rightarrow
\text{retraining}.
$$

That is a beautiful combination of ML and formal methods. ([DROPS][3])

There is also current work specifically on **probabilistic verification of neural policies**, using abstraction and refinement to establish bounds on unsafe behavior. ([AAAI Publications][4])

And probabilistic verification of neural networks can address properties such as safety and fairness under distributions of inputs. ([Proceedings of Machine Learning Research][5])

