## The deepest connection

I'd summarize the whole subject in one sentence:

> **pGCL gives you a language for reasoning mathematically about probabilistic computation; gradient-based ML gives you a way to optimize parameterized probabilistic computation; modern verified-AI research is increasingly trying to combine the two.**

And there is a particularly nice research-level progression for you:

**GCL → pGCL → expectations → weakest pre-expectations → MDPs → probabilistic inference → differentiable probabilistic programming → gradient estimators → neural networks → verified neural policies → verified/quantitative AI.**

The first half is very close to what you're already doing in Isabelle. The second half is the bridge into modern AI.

([Archive of Formal Proofs][1])

